A=[("tulsi-benefits-and-uses","Tulsi Benefits and Uses: A Practical Guide to Holy Basil","Tulsi benefits, tea and kadha recipes, safe daily use and who should avoid holy basil.",(47,107,58),
"<h2>What is tulsi?</h2><p>Tulsi (Ocimum sanctum), or holy basil, is grown in homes across India and used in Ayurveda for seasonal wellness. Its leaves are aromatic and slightly peppery.</p><h2>Traditional uses</h2><p>People commonly use tulsi for cough, sore throat and cold-season comfort, and as a calming herbal tea. Small studies suggest possible benefits for stress and blood sugar, but evidence is still limited.</p><h2>How to use it</h2><p>Simmer 8 to 10 leaves with ginger and a little honey for a simple tea. Fresh leaves can also be added to water or kadha.</p><h2>Who should be careful</h2><p>Pregnant women, people on blood thinners or diabetes medicine, and anyone with a planned surgery should ask a doctor first.</p>"),
("ashwagandha-benefits-and-side-effects","Ashwagandha Benefits and Side Effects: What to Know First","Ashwagandha benefits for stress and sleep, common doses, side effects and safety notes.",(120,90,40),
"<h2>About ashwagandha</h2><p>Ashwagandha (Withania somnifera) is a root used in Ayurveda and often called an adaptogen, meaning it may help the body handle stress.</p><h2>What research suggests</h2><p>Some trials report lower stress scores and better sleep quality. Results vary by product, dose and study quality, so it is not a guaranteed fix.</p><h2>Forms and dosing</h2><p>It is sold as powder, capsules and tea. Follow the label or a practitioner, and begin with a low dose to see how you respond.</p><h2>Side effects</h2><p>Stomach upset and drowsiness are reported. Avoid it in pregnancy, and talk to a doctor if you have thyroid, liver or autoimmune conditions.</p>"),
("giloy-kadha-benefits-and-precautions","Giloy Kadha: Benefits, Preparation and Precautions","How giloy kadha is made, its traditional benefits and who should avoid giloy.",(30,95,80),
"<h2>What is giloy?</h2><p>Giloy (Tinospora cordifolia), also called guduchi, is a climbing vine used in Ayurveda for fever, digestion and general wellness.</p><h2>Simple kadha method</h2><p>Boil a small piece of giloy stem with tulsi leaves and ginger in two cups of water until it reduces to one cup. Strain and sip warm.</p><h2>Cautions</h2><p>Giloy may lower blood sugar and can affect immune-related conditions. Reports of liver problems with some products mean you should avoid long, high-dose use and consult a doctor, especially with liver disease or autoimmune disorders.</p>"),
("amla-benefits-for-hair-skin-and-digestion","Amla Benefits for Hair, Skin and Digestion","Amla (Indian gooseberry) benefits, ways to eat it and simple hair and skin uses.",(90,120,40),
"<h2>Why amla is popular</h2><p>Amla, or Indian gooseberry, is a tart fruit valued as a natural source of vitamin C and antioxidants.</p><h2>Everyday ways to use it</h2><p>Eat it fresh, as murabba, dried candy, juice or in chyawanprash. Amla oil is a traditional scalp massage ingredient.</p><h2>What to expect</h2><p>Amla can support a balanced diet, but it will not cure hair loss or disease. Too much may upset the stomach, and people on diabetes medicine should monitor sugar levels.</p>")]
A+=[("neem-benefits-for-skin-and-oral-care","Neem Benefits for Skin and Oral Care","Neem leaf uses for skin and teeth, how to use it safely and who should avoid it.",(40,110,70),"<h2>Why neem is valued</h2><p>Neem (Azadirachta indica) has long been used in Indian homes for skin care and as a natural datun for teeth.</p><h2>Common uses</h2><p>Neem leaf paste is applied to minor blemishes, and neem water is used as a rinse. Always patch test first.</p><h2>Cautions</h2><p>Neem oil and seeds can be toxic if swallowed. Avoid neem in pregnancy and for young children.</p>"),
("triphala-benefits-and-how-to-take","Triphala Benefits and How to Take It","Triphala powder explained: ingredients, digestion support, dosage basics and cautions.",(110,80,50),"<h2>What is triphala?</h2><p>Triphala combines three fruits: amla, haritaki and bibhitaki. It is a classic Ayurvedic digestive tonic.</p><h2>How people take it</h2><p>A small spoon of powder in warm water at night is common. Start low, as it can loosen stools.</p><h2>Cautions</h2><p>Avoid in pregnancy and diarrhea, and ask a doctor if you take regular medicines.</p>"),
("brahmi-benefits-for-memory-and-focus","Brahmi Benefits for Memory and Focus","Brahmi (Bacopa) uses, what studies say about memory and focus, and safety notes.",(60,90,120),"<h2>About brahmi</h2><p>Brahmi (Bacopa monnieri) is an Ayurvedic herb traditionally linked with learning and calm.</p><h2>What studies show</h2><p>Some small trials report modest memory improvement after weeks of use, but evidence is mixed.</p><h2>Cautions</h2><p>It may cause nausea or cramps. Ask a doctor if you have thyroid issues or take sedatives.</p>"),
("turmeric-benefits-and-golden-milk","Turmeric Benefits and Golden Milk Recipe","Turmeric and curcumin benefits, a simple golden milk recipe and precautions.",(190,140,20),"<h2>The golden spice</h2><p>Turmeric contains curcumin, studied for its anti-inflammatory properties. Absorption improves with black pepper and fat.</p><h2>Golden milk</h2><p>Warm a cup of milk with half a teaspoon of turmeric, a pinch of pepper and honey to taste.</p><h2>Cautions</h2><p>High-dose supplements may affect gallstones and blood thinners. Food amounts are generally safe.</p>")]
N2=[
("mulethi-licorice-root-benefits-and-risks","Mulethi (Licorice Root): Benefits, Uses and Risks","Mulethi benefits for throat comfort, how it is used and why high blood pressure patients must be careful.","Mulethi is a sweet root found in many cough lozenges, but its sweetness hides real risks.","What mulethi is","Mulethi (Glycyrrhiza glabra) is the root of the licorice plant. Ayurveda uses it for sore throat and cough, often as tea or powder.","How it is used","Small pieces are chewed or simmered in water. Short-term use in modest amounts is typical.","Risks to know","Regular high intake can raise blood pressure and lower potassium. Avoid it in pregnancy and heart or kidney disease unless a doctor agrees.","Mulethi can soothe, but it is a herb to use briefly and carefully."),
("ginger-benefits-for-nausea-and-digestion","Ginger Benefits for Nausea and Digestion","Ginger benefits explained: nausea, digestion, easy tea recipe and safety limits.","Ginger is one of the few kitchen herbs with fairly consistent evidence behind it.","Evidence for nausea","Several studies suggest ginger can reduce nausea, including in pregnancy and after chemotherapy, but pregnant readers should confirm amounts with a doctor.","Simple ginger tea","Simmer a few thin slices in a cup of water for 5 to 10 minutes, strain and sip.","Side effects","Large amounts may cause heartburn or bleeding risk with blood thinners.","Ginger is a safe everyday spice for most people, and a useful place to start."),
("fenugreek-methi-seeds-benefits","Fenugreek (Methi) Seeds: Benefits and Cautions","Methi seeds benefits for blood sugar, digestion and lactation, with dosage cautions.","Soaked methi seeds are an old Indian habit, but how much do they really help?","What studies suggest","Some trials show modest improvements in blood sugar with fenugreek, though study sizes are small.","How people use it","Seeds are soaked overnight and eaten or the water is drunk in the morning.","Cautions","It can cause gas and may lower blood sugar too much alongside diabetes drugs. Avoid large amounts in pregnancy.","Methi works best as part of a balanced diet, not as a replacement for diabetes care."),
("aloe-vera-benefits-skin-and-juice","Aloe Vera: Benefits for Skin and Is Juice Safe?","Aloe vera gel for burns and skin, and why aloe juice and latex need care.","Aloe vera gel and aloe juice are not the same thing, and the difference matters.","Gel for skin","Pure aloe gel is widely used on minor sunburn and dry skin. Patch test first.","Drinking aloe","Products containing aloe latex can act as a strong laxative. Choose decolorized, reputable products and avoid long use.","Who should avoid it","Pregnant or breastfeeding women and people with bowel or kidney problems should skip oral aloe.","Aloe is great on the skin, but be far more careful before swallowing it."),
("shatavari-benefits-for-women","Shatavari: Traditional Uses and What Research Says","Shatavari traditional uses for women's health, evidence level and safety.","Shatavari is often marketed as a women's tonic, so it is worth checking the claims.","Traditional role","Ayurveda uses shatavari (Asparagus racemosus) as a general rejuvenating herb, especially for women.","Evidence level","Human research is limited, and claims about hormones or lactation are not firmly proven.","Safety","Avoid with hormone-sensitive conditions or pregnancy unless advised by a doctor.","Treat shatavari as a traditional herb with promising but unproven benefits."),
("moringa-drumstick-leaves-benefits","Moringa Leaves: Nutrition, Benefits and Cautions","Moringa nutrition, how to eat drumstick leaves and powder, and who should be careful.","Moringa leaves are called a superfood, but food use is not the same as supplement use.","Nutrition","Moringa leaves provide protein, iron and vitamins, and are eaten as a vegetable in many Indian homes.","How to eat","Add leaves to dal or parathas, or use a small amount of powder in smoothies.","Cautions","Roots and bark are not advised in pregnancy. Concentrated powders may interact with medicines.","Moringa is best enjoyed as food first and supplement second."),
("cinnamon-benefits-and-cassia-warning","Cinnamon Benefits and the Cassia Coumarin Warning","Cinnamon benefits for blood sugar, the difference between Ceylon and cassia, and safe use.","Not all cinnamon sold in shops is the same, and one type has a safety limit.","Ceylon vs cassia","True cinnamon is Ceylon. Most cheap cinnamon is cassia, which contains more coumarin.","Blood sugar research","Results are mixed, so cinnamon should not replace diabetes treatment.","Safe use","Use it as a spice. Large daily amounts of cassia may stress the liver.","Enjoy cinnamon in food and choose Ceylon if you use it often."),
("pudina-mint-benefits-and-tea","Pudina (Mint) Benefits, Tea and Chutney Ideas","Mint benefits for digestion and freshness, simple tea recipe and who should be careful.","Mint is cooling, cheap and already in most kitchens.","Digestive comfort","Peppermint oil capsules have evidence for irritable bowel symptoms, while fresh mint tea is a mild, pleasant option.","Easy mint tea","Steep a handful of fresh leaves in hot water for 5 minutes.","Cautions","Mint may worsen acid reflux. Do not apply strong peppermint oil near the faces of babies.","Mint is a gentle everyday herb with a few specific cautions."),
("chamomile-tea-and-herbal-teas-for-sleep","Herbal Teas for Sleep: Chamomile and Others","Herbal teas for sleep: chamomile, tulsi and others, with realistic expectations.","A warm cup of tea at night may help your routine more than any single herb.","Chamomile","Small studies link chamomile with slightly better sleep quality, though results are not strong.","Other options","Lemon balm and tulsi are also used for relaxation.","Cautions","Chamomile can trigger allergy in people sensitive to ragweed. Avoid caffeinated teas late.","A calming ritual and good sleep habits matter most."),
("how-to-grow-a-herb-garden-at-home","How to Grow a Herb Garden at Home","Start a home herb garden with tulsi, mint, aloe and curry leaves: sunlight, soil and tips.","You do not need a garden to grow fresh herbs, only a sunny window.","Pick easy herbs","Tulsi, mint, coriander, aloe and curry leaf grow well in pots.","Light and water","Most herbs need 4 to 6 hours of sun and well-drained soil. Water when the top soil feels dry.","Harvesting","Pick leaves regularly to encourage bushy growth, and avoid pesticides on herbs you eat.","A few pots can give you fresh, trustworthy herbs year-round."),
("herb-drug-interactions-guide","Herb and Medicine Interactions: What You Must Know","Herb and drug interactions explained: blood thinners, diabetes drugs and how to stay safe.","Herbs act on the body, so they can clash with medicines.","Common risks","Ginger, giloy, garlic and turmeric supplements can affect bleeding or blood sugar.","How to reduce risk","Keep a list of every herb and medicine you take and share it with your doctor or pharmacist.","Before surgery","Stop supplements as advised, because some can affect bleeding or anesthesia.","When in doubt, ask before you combine."),
("home-remedies-for-cough-and-cold","Home Remedies for Cough and Cold That Are Reasonable","Safe home remedies for cough and cold: fluids, honey, tulsi tea and when to see a doctor.","Most colds pass on their own, and simple comfort measures help.","What helps","Warm fluids, rest, steam and honey in warm water can ease symptoms in adults and older children.","Honey warning","Never give honey to babies under 12 months.","See a doctor","Seek care for breathing difficulty, high fever, chest pain or symptoms beyond about 10 days.","Home care is for mild illness, not warning signs."),
("curry-leaves-benefits-for-hair-and-health","Curry Leaves: Benefits for Hair and Health","Curry leaf benefits, ways to eat them and what to expect for hair care.","Curry leaves are a daily kitchen staple with a big hair-care reputation.","Nutrition","They contribute small amounts of iron, calcium and antioxidants when eaten regularly.","Hair claims","Evidence that curry leaves regrow hair is limited. A balanced diet matters more.","How to use","Add fresh leaves to cooking, chutney or buttermilk.","Enjoy them for flavor and nutrition, with realistic hair expectations."),
("fennel-saunf-benefits-for-digestion","Fennel (Saunf) Benefits for Digestion","Saunf benefits as a post-meal digestive, fennel tea and safety notes.","The after-meal saunf habit has a sensible reason behind it.","Digestive use","Fennel seeds are traditionally chewed to ease gas and bloating, with some supportive research.","Fennel tea","Crush half a teaspoon of seeds and steep in hot water for 10 minutes.","Cautions","Rare allergies occur, and concentrated fennel oil is not for children or pregnancy.","A pinch of saunf after meals is a low-risk habit for most people."),
("ajwain-carom-seeds-benefits","Ajwain (Carom Seeds): Benefits and Uses","Ajwain benefits for gas and digestion, ajwain water and safety notes.","Ajwain is the go-to home remedy for gas in many Indian kitchens.","Traditional use","Ajwain (Trachyspermum ammi) is used for bloating and indigestion.","Ajwain water","Boil half a teaspoon of seeds in a cup of water, strain and sip warm.","Cautions","It is strong and heating, so avoid excess in pregnancy and acidity-prone people.","Ajwain works as an occasional kitchen remedy, not a daily medicine.")]
